<ArticleLayout
	title="Replaying 55 Historical Setups: One Comparison — Semantic Signal"
	description="An offline replay of the bot's own recorded cycles through two prompt versions and two model generations: what matched, what did not, and what such a replay can and cannot show."
	meta="Article · published October 2026 · replay run 2026-09-18"
>
	<span slot="title">Replaying 55 historical setups: one comparison</span>
	<p slot="subtitle" class="subtitle">The engine keeps every cycle it has ever run. This is what happened when a run of those cycles was replayed through two prompt versions instead of trusted as history.</p>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>What was replayed</h2>
		<p>Every cycle is recorded: the prompt that was sent, the model's answer, the parsed decision and the market context. The replay took <strong>55 recorded setups</strong> — 45 cycles that historically produced an entry (BUY or SELL) and 10 that produced a CLOSE — and sent each one back through the pipeline with a fixed prompt.</p>
		<p>Three variants, all on the same replay data:</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>K</strong> — the prompt version in use before the rewrite, on the model generation of the time;</li>
			<li><strong>W</strong> — the current prompt, on the same model generation;</li>
			<li><strong>W36</strong> — the current prompt, on the previous model generation.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Each variant produced 55 calls, 165 in total, run once, on 2026-09-18.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>What was measured</h2>
		<p>Two things only: whether the variant <strong>entered</strong> on a historical entry setup, and whether it chose the <strong>same direction</strong> as the historical decision. No trade outcome, P&amp;L, slippage or fee was scored — the replay does not know what would have happened, only what each variant said.</p>
		<table class="data">
			<thead>
				<tr><th>Variant</th><th>Entries / 45</th><th>Same direction</th><th>Decisions</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>K — old prompt</td><td>0 (0%)</td><td>0</td><td>HOLD 45</td></tr>
				<tr><td>W — current prompt</td><td>9 (20%)</td><td>9</td><td>HOLD 36, BUY 5, SELL 4</td></tr>
				<tr><td>W36 — current prompt, older model</td><td>15 (33%)</td><td>15</td><td>HOLD 30, BUY 11, SELL 4</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>On the 10 CLOSE setups the same difference appears: the old prompt produced CLOSE 2, UPDATE 2 and HOLD 6; the current prompt produced HOLD 10.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>Where the variants disagree</h2>
		<p>Comparing the two model generations under the same current prompt: <strong>31 of 45</strong> decisions matched. Of the disagreements, 10 are cases where the older model entered and the newer one held, and 4 where the newer one entered and the older one held; the remaining agreement includes 5 setups where both entered in the same direction.</p>
		<p>That matrix is the most useful part of the result: changing the model moved more decisions than the prompt rewrite did, and neither change was evaluated against an outcome.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>Run quality</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>165 calls, 0 failures, 0 retries required;</li>
			<li>every response finished normally (<code>finishReason: STOP</code>), no truncation;</li>
			<li>mean call latency <strong>20.4 s</strong>.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>Those numbers only say the replay itself behaved; they say nothing about the quality of the decisions.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>What it established</h2>
		<p>One concrete finding: <strong>the old prompt did not enter on any of the 45 historical entry setups</strong>, while the current one entered on 9. Whatever else is true, the rewrite changed behaviour from "almost never act" to "act sometimes". That is a calibration change, and calibration is not skill.</p>
		<p>It also made the model-generation difference visible and measurable, which is the reason to keep recorded cycles at all: without them, the only comparison available would be a memory of how the bot used to feel.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>What it does not establish</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>No outcomes were scored.</strong> Entering more often is not better; it is only different. This replay cannot say which variant would have made money.</li>
			<li><strong>The sample is small and from one period.</strong> 55 setups, one instrument, one language model family.</li>
			<li><strong>One run per variant.</strong> No repeats, so the run-to-run variance of the model is not measured.</li>
			<li><strong>Historical setups are not a neutral test.</strong> They were selected because the bot acted on them at the time; the replay deliberately excludes the cycles where it did nothing.</li>
			<li><strong>The replay scripts are not in the public repository.</strong> The numbers here are transcribed from the run's summary output; the method is described above so it can be repeated, but this page is currently the only published record of it.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

